    <!-- 11 FAQ -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="faq">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">11 &mdash; FAQ</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Frequently asked questions</h2>

        {[
          { q: "What equipment do I need to start UGC?", a: "Strictly speaking, just your smartphone. That is it. Any phone from the last 3-4 years shoots video good enough for UGC. For your first meaningful investment (~$100), get a ring light ($30), a wired lavalier microphone ($20), and a phone tripod ($15). This combination produces professional-quality UGC content." },
          { q: "Do I need a camera for UGC?", a: "No. The vast majority of professional UGC creators shoot on their phones. Smartphones from 2021 and later produce video quality that exceeds what social platforms require (platforms compress video during upload anyway). A dedicated camera only makes sense for YouTube long-form content or premium branded work where clients specifically request higher production value." },
          { q: "What is the single best equipment purchase for a new UGC creator?", a: "A wired lavalier microphone ($15-$20). Audio quality is the single biggest differentiator between amateur and professional-looking UGC. Bad audio causes viewers to swipe away immediately, regardless of how good the visuals are. A $20 lav mic plugged into your phone produces dramatically better audio than the built-in phone microphone." },
          { q: "Is a ring light or LED panel better?", a: "A ring light ($30-$50) is better for most beginners because it provides even, flattering light with minimal setup. An LED panel ($80-$150) provides more professional and natural-looking light with better shadow definition, but requires more careful positioning. Start with a ring light, upgrade to an LED panel when you want more control over your lighting." },
          { q: "What editing app should I use for UGC?", a: "CapCut is the industry standard for UGC editing. It is free, has auto-captions, templates, effects, and direct TikTok integration. The learning curve is minimal. For creators wanting more control, VN Video Editor (free) offers a desktop-like timeline on mobile. For desktop editing, DaVinci Resolve is free and professional-grade." },
          { q: "How important is the background in UGC?", a: "Important but not complicated. A clean, uncluttered background signals professionalism. You do not need a studio — a plain wall, bookshelf, or tidy corner of a room works perfectly. Avoid messy bedrooms, cluttered desks, or distracting backgrounds. A simple backdrop stand with fabric ($30-$50) provides a consistent background for all your content." },
          { q: "Should I invest in a teleprompter?", a: "Not initially. Most UGC content works better with natural, conversational delivery rather than reading from a script. If you find yourself struggling with talking points, a free teleprompter app on a tablet ($0) works well enough. Physical teleprompters ($100-$300) are for creators who produce daily scripted content and need speed." },
          { q: "What microphone works best for cooking or fitness UGC?", a: "A wireless lavalier microphone. Wired mics restrict movement, and phone mics pick up too much ambient noise when you are moving around. The DJI Mic Mini ($100), Hollyland Lark M2 ($100), or Rode Wireless GO II ($250) give you clear audio while moving freely. Clip the transmitter to your collar and move naturally." },
          { q: "Can I do UGC with an older phone?", a: "Yes, if it is from 2020 or later and shoots at least 1080p video. Older phones may have weaker low-light performance, so good lighting becomes even more important. The $100 setup (ring light + lav mic + tripod) compensates for older phone cameras effectively. If your phone is from before 2019, the camera quality may be a limitation." },
          { q: "How much should I spend on equipment as a new UGC creator?", a: "Start at $0 and create your first 10-20 pieces of content. Then invest $100 in the essentials (ring light, lav mic, tripod). Only spend more after you are earning from UGC and have identified specific limitations in your current setup. The $100 tier is sufficient for 90% of UGC work." },
        ].map((item) => (
